HashSet<>();
___________

- HashSet class Implements SET Interface

KeyFeatures of SET Interface :

✅ No duplicate elements allowed.
✅ Unordered [ Doses not maintain insertion order ]
✅ Allows Only ONE Null Value
✅ Uses HashMap Internally

📌 Internal Working of HashSet
When you create a HashSet, it actually creates an empty HashMap internally
and stores each element as a key in that HashMap, using a dummy value (PRESENT).

HashSet<String> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add("Anand");
set.add("Kulkarni");

// Internally, it is stored as:
HashMap<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("Anand", PRESENT);
map.put("Kulkarni", PRESENT);


Here, PRESENT is a dummy placeholder used internally by Java’s HashSet implementation.

2️⃣ What is the default capacity of HashSet?
✔️ HashSet internally uses a HashMap with a default initial capacity of 16 and a load factor of 0.75.

3️⃣ What happens when we add a duplicate element in HashSet?
✔️ It gets ignored because HashSet checks for duplicates using HashMap.containsKey().
4️⃣ Can HashSet store null values?
✔️ Yes, HashSet allows one null value because HashMap allows one null key.
5️⃣ Why is HashSet unordered?
✔️ Because it uses hashing, and hash functions do not maintain order.


✅ HashSet uses a HashMap internally, storing elements as keys and using a dummy value (PRESENT).
✅ No duplicates allowed (because HashMap does not allow duplicate keys).
✅ Unordered storage due to hashing.
✅ Fast performance (O(1) for add, remove, contains).
✅ Allows null, but only one.

