** LinkedHashSet<>();

- LinkedHashSet is a child class of HashSet and maintains insertion order.
- It is implemented using a combination of HashMap and Doubly Linked List internally.

java.lang.Object
 └── java.util.Collection<E>
     └── java.util.Set<E>
         └── java.util.HashSet<E>
             └── java.util.LinkedHashSet<E>



2️⃣ Key Features
✅ Maintains insertion order (unlike HashSet).
✅ Does not allow duplicate elements.
✅ It Allows Only one Null Value.
✅ Uses a doubly linked list along with HashMap for internal storage.
✅ Better iteration performance than HashSet (since it maintains order).
✅ Not thread-safe (like all standard collection classes).


5️⃣ When to Use LinkedHashSet?
✅ When you need a Set that preserves insertion order.
✅ When you require fast lookups (O(1) time complexity like HashSet).
✅ When iteration speed matters (since it’s faster than HashSet for iteration).






6️⃣ Difference Between HashSet and LinkedHashSet
Feature   	              HashSet	                        LinkedHashSet
Ordering	              No insertion order	            Maintains insertion order
Performance	              Faster for inserts/deletes	    Slightly slower (due to linked list overhead)
Internal Structure	      HashMap	                        LinkedHashMap (HashMap + Doubly Linked List)


